


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: ory
@title: When Did Musicians Cross the Alps? Renaissance Mobility and the Problem of Fragmentary Data
@presenter: ory
@abstract: 

Renaissance musicians regularly crossed the Alps, traveling
from the Low Countries and German-speaking lands to Italy,
Hungary, and beyond. Yet for all that we know about the
biographies of these so-called <i>Oltremontani</i>, we know little
about the practical realities of their travel. Archival
records rarely document individual people—let alone,
musicians—at specific Alpine crossings, and customs
accounts tend to record goods rather than travelers. The
question nevertheless matters for musicology: Alpine
mobility shaped patterns of employment, repertorial
circulation, institutional exchange, and the transmission
of musical practices across Europe.

This presentation argues that the best way to approach this
problem is by assembling a wide range of circumstantial
evidence about musicians’ lives and movements. I draw on
data collected through <i>Mapping the Musical Renaissance</i>
(https://renaissancemapping.org), a digital project that
models the movements of Renaissance musicians through
structured biographical data. I then consider how
Retrieval-Augmented Generation might help mediate between
several forms of evidence: structured event data,
incomplete musician biographies, the prose entries of the
<i>Prosopography of Renaissance Singers</i>
(https://ricercar.pcr.cesr.univ-tours.fr), and secondary
literature on Alpine routes, seasonality, and transit
infrastructure.

Rather than treating AI as a source of historical answers,
the paper asks whether it can function as a tool for
assembling evidence, identifying plausible routes and
temporal patterns, surfacing contradictions, and clarifying
degrees of uncertainty. The broader aim is methodological:
to explore how digital musicology might employ AI to
formulate historically-responsible hypotheses from
fragmentary mobility data while keeping humanistic judgment
at the center of interpretation.

@@END: PRESENTATION



